      "q": "What age is best for logic puzzles for ESL kids?",
      "a": "Children as young as 4 can start with simple picture-based puzzles. For ages 4-6, use matching and sequencing puzzles with images. Ages 7-12 can handle word-based deduction puzzles. Always start easy to build confidence."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do logic puzzles help with English learning?",
      "a": "Logic puzzles require kids to understand instructions in English, solve problems, and often explain their answers. This builds vocabulary, comprehension, and sentence structure naturally. The repetition of common phrases also reinforces language patterns."
    },
    {
      "q": "How long should a 6-year-old spend on a logic puzzle?",
      "a": "Aim for 5-10 minutes per puzzle for ages 4-6. If it takes longer, the puzzle might be too difficult. For ages 7-12, 10-20 minutes is ideal. Use a timer to track progress and keep it fun."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can logic puzzles replace English lessons?",
      "a": "No, logic puzzles are a supplement, not a replacement. They work best alongside regular English practice like reading, speaking, and writing. Use puzzles to reinforce vocabulary and critical thinking in a playful way."
    },
    {
      "q": "What type of logic puzzle is best for vocabulary building?",
      "a": "Categorization puzzles are excellent for vocabulary. For example, sorting animals into groups like 'farm' vs. 'wild' teaches thematic word sets. Sequencing puzzles also help with ordering words like 'first', 'then', 'finally'."
    }
